  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT SUN SEP 27 2026\n\nThis evening, surface ridging continues to be in place across\nmuch of Eastern Conus and centered over the Mississippi River\nValley. In the upper levels, a weakening ridge is still present\nacross Mexico with a large low just centered over New Jersey with\nnorthwest flow into Alabama. In the short term, look for one more\nday of pleasant temperatures along with low humidity and rain\nfree conditions with northerly low level winds. By Tuesday, the\nsurface ridge center will shift eastward some to over Ohio/ \nKentucky with a more easterly flow around it into Alabama. By \nWednesday, the surface ridge center should nudge further eastward,\nto over the Virginia/North Carolina area. This will create a \nsoutheast low level flow into Alabama. The changes Tuesday and \nWednesday will result in moderating temperatures and dew points as\nthe week progresses. In the upper levels on Tuesday, a bit of \nenergy is expected to pull off from the main low over New Jersey \nas it pushes eastward into the Atlantic and form a closed low over\nthe Gulf by Thursday. At the same time Tuesday, the upper ridge \nover Mexico will get infringed upon by a low moving eastward out \nof California into New Mexico. Moisture will need to recover a bit\nbefore we can expect any significant rain chances. Low rain chances\nreturn by Thursday for portions of the area. However, more \nsubstantial chances hold off until next weekend ahead of an \napproaching front.\n\n08",
